1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 We're going to kick off our discussions on manufacturing processes for metals by talking about

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:07,440
 casting and forging, some of the oldest techniques for metalworking.

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,400
 Interestingly, these techniques are still extensively used today,

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,360
 both in the production of standard sections of the kinds of steel's

5
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:15,600
 and aluminiums that you can buy to make things out of,

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:19,200
 but also in the large scale production of engineering components.

7
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,440
 Casting is a manufacturing technique where molten metal is poured into a mould,

8
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:27,040
 which is a hollow cavity of the geometry that you'd like to make.

9
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:29,920
 After the molten metal is poured, it's allowed to cool and solidify

10
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:32,080
 inside of the mould, and when the mould is removed,

11
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:35,360
 you're left with a solid positive of the part that you're looking for.

12
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:37,200
 While you can cast lots of different materials,

13
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,200
 and we'll talk a little bit more about casting of, say,

14
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,120
 engineering polymers in two weeks' time,

15
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:44,400
 this video's going to be primarily focused on casting of metals.

16
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,760
 There are all sorts of unique challenges that need to be overcome when casting metals,

17
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:50,080
 not least of which is that you need to heat your metals up,

18
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:52,240
 pretty hot to get them liquid in the first place.

19
00:00:52,240 --> 00:00:55,280
 But we've gotten pretty good at it over the last 7,000 years,

20
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:57,840
 some of the earliest metals to be cast being copper alloys,

21
00:00:57,840 --> 00:01:02,240
 owing to their low melting temperatures and their relative good behaviour in the liquid state,

22
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:04,960
 and the ease by which copper can be mined and refined.

23
00:01:04,960 --> 00:01:07,920
 Casting can be a very economical way to produce metal parts,

24
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:09,200
 especially at volume.

25
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:12,160
 Depending on the quality and durability of the mould that you're using,

26
00:01:12,160 --> 00:01:16,000
 you can generally pull many, many parts from a single upfront investment.

27
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,560
 You can make really complicated geometries using this technique

28
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:21,440
 with very little additional machining or clean-up required.

29
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,600
 You waste very little material in this process

30
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,840
 because it's kind of an additive process.

31
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:27,840
 You put in as much material as you need.

32
00:01:27,840 --> 00:01:29,680
 You never really need to remove any.

33
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:31,600
 And because you're working with the liquid metal,

34
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:34,560
 you can perform casting at the point of refinement if you like,

35
00:01:34,560 --> 00:01:36,240
 reducing transport costs.

36
00:01:36,240 --> 00:01:37,520
 This isn't the only way to do it.

37
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:40,000
 A bulk metals can be cast into ingots and often are,

38
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,040
 and then sent off to specific casting plants to be remelted

39
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:45,120
 and casting to their final geometries.

40
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:48,960
 Broadly speaking, casting processes are classified as expendable or non-expendable,

41
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:52,480
 and really this comes down to the expected lifetime of the mould.

42
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:57,200
 There are some processes like sand casting that have very expendable single-use moulds.

43
00:01:57,200 --> 00:01:59,360
 Here, the negative mould is constructed from sand

44
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:01,920
 around the positive geometry of the part you'd like to make.

45
00:02:01,920 --> 00:02:05,840
 This can be quite economical as you don't need to invest a lot of money into making a mould.

46
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:09,600
 While you do need a positive of the part to be able to make a mould when sand casting,

47
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:14,000
 this positive doesn't need to be the same material as what you want your final finished product to be.

48
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,520
 You could, for example, 3D print a positive of the geometry that you'd like.

49
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:20,160
 Use this to make the negative in the sand casting mould,

50
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,560
 and then pour your moulds and metal into the mould.

51
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:25,760
 The final result, the product you pull from the mould, will be a metal product,

52
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:28,480
 even though you started with a plastic positive to begin with.

53
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:31,760
 Now, by their very nature, the moulds when sand casting are expendable,

54
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:35,680
 they're destroyed when you remove the part from them, but they're reasonably easy to produce.

55
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:39,360
 Though there is obviously a labor cost involved in producing the mould every time,

56
00:02:39,360 --> 00:02:42,640
 this means that for low volume run, sand casting makes a lot of sense.

57
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:46,160
 When you want to make thousands or tens of thousands or millions of parts, however,

58
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:49,440
 you're probably going to reach for a non-expendable casting process.

59
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:54,000
 For example, die casting involves injecting molten metal into a very expensive,

60
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:57,680
 machined, super duper accurate, hardened steel mould.

61
00:02:57,680 --> 00:03:00,080
 The upfront investment costs for this process are much higher,

62
00:03:00,080 --> 00:03:03,040
 both in the infrastructure that you need to be able to inject the molten metal,

63
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:05,280
 but also in the production of these dies.

64
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:08,800
 Unlike sand casting, where you can just take a positive geometry that could be, for example,

65
00:03:08,800 --> 00:03:11,600
 3D printed and you impress this into some sand.

66
00:03:11,600 --> 00:03:15,040
 When die casting, you need to machine the mould ahead of time.

67
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:18,720
 While generally speaking, the end result from die casting is likely to be much nicer than that

68
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:22,160
 from sand casting, both in terms of the surface finish and the quality and the

69
00:03:22,160 --> 00:03:27,840
 dimensional accuracy, but also in terms of the lack of inclusions and air bubbles that you might get.

70
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,240
 This comes at a cost, a real physical cost.

71
00:03:30,240 --> 00:03:33,760
 Die casting is normally much, much more expensive than sand casting.

72
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:36,000
 Of course, at the production scales that you're working at,

73
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,160
 this upfront cost is recovered reasonably quickly compared to having to remake the mould

74
00:03:40,160 --> 00:03:41,760
 every time when sand casting.

75
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:45,760
 Casting is a very diverse process, and there are many, many metals that can be cast.

76
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:50,000
 So you do need to be a little bit careful about the thermal diffusivity of a metal when casting.

77
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,720
 As we talked about last week, particularly for ions and steels,

78
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:56,000
 the cooling rate can have a significant effect on the microstructures that form.

79
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:59,200
 This means you need to be very careful about the choice of material for your mould,

80
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,800
 the choice of material that you're casting, the temperatures that you perform the casting

81
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:05,680
 process at and the conditions in the ambient environment.

82
00:04:05,680 --> 00:04:09,120
 Casting is still too quickly might result in a product which is hard and brittle.

83
00:04:09,120 --> 00:04:12,480
 Casting it too slowly might result in a product that's far too soft for use.

84
00:04:12,480 --> 00:04:16,240
 Of course, many steel products you can heat treat after casting to achieve your desired

85
00:04:16,240 --> 00:04:17,200
 microstructure.

86
00:04:17,200 --> 00:04:20,880
 Design of moulds and dies and casting processes is obviously a field of its own,

87
00:04:20,880 --> 00:04:22,640
 and we won't get too much detail here.

88
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:26,480
 What you should be aware of is that the word casting encompasses processes which are relatively

89
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:31,040
 simple that you can do in your backyard, all the way up to processes that are well and truly

90
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:36,000
 outside the reach of most engineers and only really make sense at large production scales.

91
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,520
 That brings us nicely to talking about forging, which is also kind of an umbrella term

92
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:42,000
 for a bunch of different manufacturing processes.

93
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,880
 The thing that they all have in common is that they generally involve working a metal

94
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:46,320
 to shape under compression.

95
00:04:46,320 --> 00:04:50,640
 Very often forging is aided by the application of heat which temporarily reduces the yield

96
00:04:50,640 --> 00:04:52,880
 strength of a material and makes it more pliable.

97
00:04:52,880 --> 00:04:56,320
 And like casting, forging operates at both ends of the spectrum.

98
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,600
 On the one hand, when I say forging, you might be picturing a blacksmith,

99
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,600
 striking a piece of hot metal with a hammer.

100
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,360
 And this is certainly an example of forging.

101
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:05,440
 Many early forge parts were made this way.

102
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:09,200
 On the other hand, when I say forging, you might be picturing a humongous hydraulic press,

103
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,360
 working massive pieces of steel into shape.

104
00:05:11,440 --> 00:05:14,160
 While there are still modern blacksmiths who forge things by hand,

105
00:05:14,160 --> 00:05:17,120
 these days usually aided with hydraulic power hammers.

106
00:05:17,120 --> 00:05:20,000
 If you're designing an engineering component to be forged, it's likely this is going to

107
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,280
 happen as part of some automated or semi-automated process.

108
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:26,960
 Typically, engineering components are forged using a die, which has a similar meaning in this

109
00:05:26,960 --> 00:05:30,320
 context as that for casting and negative of the geometry that you'd like.

110
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:33,040
 Usually, the metals are heated up and then pressed into a die,

111
00:05:33,040 --> 00:05:36,400
 or the die is pressed over them to force them into shape.

112
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:40,480
 Obviously, making the solar metal flow like this into the shape of the die requires a tremendous

113
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:44,640
 amount of force, and this process is normally aided by hydraulic powered machines

114
00:05:44,640 --> 00:05:46,880
 and application of heat on the metals to soften them.

115
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:49,840
 But generally speaking, the amount of power that you require, the size of the machine you

116
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:52,320
 require depends on the size of the part you'd like to make.

117
00:05:52,320 --> 00:05:55,440
 The complexity of the geometry and the difference between that geometry and the shape that

118
00:05:55,440 --> 00:05:58,160
 you're starting with, the material properties of the metal that you're forging,

119
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,000
 particularly its yield strength and temperature,

120
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,720
 and the speed at which you want to conduct a forging process.

121
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:07,120
 Generally speaking, as you forge larger jobs that are further in their final geometry than

122
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:11,600
 they're starting geometry that are of higher strength or hardness at lower temperatures,

123
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:16,160
 and more quickly, the more and more power that you require on your machine to make this happen.

124
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:20,160
 For this reason, the scale of some forging machines is just absolutely wild.

125
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:24,000
 Not all forging processes are performed hot, in fact many fasteners, for example,

126
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,120
 are forged cold.

127
00:06:25,120 --> 00:06:28,720
 With only a large initial investment required to make the die and set up the tooling in the

128
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:32,560
 machinery, this process can be highly automated and is one of the reasons why fasteners

129
00:06:32,560 --> 00:06:33,360
 are so cheap.

130
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:36,400
 You should be aware that while casting and forging are talked about in the same context

131
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:40,000
 and they're both suitable for large-scale production, there's a significant difference

132
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:42,080
 in the types of microstructures that they form.

133
00:06:42,080 --> 00:06:45,920
 Casting, by virtue of starting at the liquid state and allowing the metal to cool inside of

134
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:50,400
 the mold, generally results in a reasonably homogenous and randomized grain structure.

135
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:54,560
 Forging, on the other hand, has the tendency to deform grains and introduce a significant texture

136
00:06:54,560 --> 00:06:56,400
 in the materials that you're working with.

137
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:59,760
 This might sound bad, but actually, generally speaking, the texture that you induce

138
00:06:59,760 --> 00:07:02,400
 ends up being the shape of the part you're trying to make.

139
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:05,920
 And in most applications, this actually results in a strengthening of the geometry of

140
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,160
 that part in the directions you care about.

141
00:07:08,160 --> 00:07:12,320
 As a result, forged components are typically very anisotropic and usually this is actually a

142
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:12,800
 good thing.

143
00:07:12,800 --> 00:07:16,480
 They're effectively much stronger and stiffer in the direction that they're intended to be loaded.

144
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:18,080
 I'll leave it there for casting and forging.

145
00:07:18,080 --> 00:07:22,240
 I've left some links to some excellent how it's made videos on both casting and forging

146
00:07:22,240 --> 00:07:23,520
 in the textbook page for this one.

147
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:26,560
 And I strongly encourage you to check them out because it'll tie everything we've just

148
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:29,280
 talked about together and you'll see it done on an actual part.
